":[{"type":"text","value":"Polls on certain topics are conducted routinely in order to monitor changes in the public’s opinions over time. One such topic is the death penalty. In 2003 a poll estimated that 64% of U.S. adults support the death penalty for a person convicted of murder. In a more recent poll, 675 out of 1,000 U.S. adults chosen at random were in favor of the death penalty for convicted murderers. Do the results of this poll provide evidence that the proportion of U.S. adults who support the death penalty for convicted murderers (p) ","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"YnH3jkl1Zv"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"changed","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"bW4kI7ODoc"}],"key":"qzkh6q3bd5"},{"type":"text","value":" between 2003 and the later poll?","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"scYzkvVo8n"}],"key":"qyCqlwlDne"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here is a figure that displays the information, as well as the question of interest:","position":{"start":{"line":64,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"wm6xnT9Jig"}],"key":"dQRv7VgIRO"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m15-prop-deathpenalt-8ec46f10aa883d40a0cd3489b08424d6.svg","alt":"A large circle represents the population of U.S. adults. We want to know p, the proportion who support the death penalty, and whether p has changed since 2003 when it was 0.64. A smaller circle represents a random sample of 1,000 U.S. adults, of which 675 are in favor.","key":"yB37SEGvRv","urlSource":"images/gen/m15-prop-deathpenalty.svg"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"l3gOoi57IS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Again, we can formulate the null and alternative hypotheses in terms of p, the proportion of U.S. adults who support the death penalty for convicted murderers:","position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"key":"YqcrTiioDZ"}],"key":"NdlIW2Hu8J"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"vdWVMbjNyB"},{"type":"text","value":": ","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"Owft4VOpoU"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"p = 0.64","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0.64</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.64</span></span></span></span>","key":"cUc3ndO3CY"},{"type":"text","value":" (no change from 2003)","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"knePUHtWaY"}],"key":"OuBzRydiTY"}],"key":"paXGEEaMis"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_a","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"GwfSRLUuZi"},{"type":"text","value":": ","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"SSAf5qsPd2"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"p \\neq 0.64","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p \\neq 0.64</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.64</span></span></span></span>","key":"cwPDu3pLRE"},{"type":"text","value":" (some change since 2003)","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"Xs8DY95dj7"}],"key":"QrWHAGpgic"}],"key":"jZ5Sc9VyXI"}],"key":"wrHAqldEIV"}],"class":"tip","key":"dLEUPGbm8F"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Setting Up a Test for a Proportion","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"Iwwcxju1HR"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-setting-up-a-test-for-a-proportion","label":"Check Your Understanding: Setting Up a Test for a Proportion","html_id":"check-your-understanding-setting-up-a-test-for-a-proportion","implicit":true,"key":"GfevQaV8KX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"According to the American Association of Community Colleges, 23% of community college students receive federal grants. The California Community College Chancellor’s Office anticipates that the percentage is smaller for California community college students. They collect a sample of 1,000 community college students in California and find that 210 received federal grants.","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"S8cgvpx0cp"}],"key":"IE3zZRjlNv"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"What is the population of interest in this study?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"All community college students in California","feedback":"Correct! The conclusion is about all California community college students; the 1,000 students are the sample."},{"correct":false,"text":"The 1,000 students in the study","feedback":"This is the sample, not the population."},{"correct":false,"text":"All community college students in the United States","feedback":"The 23% figure describes community college students nationwide—the baseline, not the population being studied."}],"multi":false,"hint":"About which group does the Chancellor's Office want to draw a conclusion?","explanation":""},"key":"fHz1xXDpA4"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the appropriate hypotheses?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.23$; $H_a$: $p < 0.23$","feedback":"Correct! $H_0$: $p = 0.23$ (same as the national rate); $H_a$: $p < 0.23$ (smaller, as anticipated)."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.23$; $H_a$: $p \\neq 0.23$","feedback":"The alternative should be one-sided (smaller), since the office specifically anticipates a smaller percentage."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.21$; $H_a$: $p < 0.21$","feedback":"The null value should be the claimed baseline 0.23, not the sample result 0.21."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The null value is the national rate, 0.23; the office anticipates a SMALLER percentage in California.","explanation":""},"key":"TfIcUgejmM"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"What is the sample proportion p-hat in this study?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"0.21","feedback":"Correct! p-hat = $210/1000 = 0.21$."},{"correct":false,"text":"0.23","feedback":"0.23 is the national (null) value, not the sample result."},{"correct":false,"text":"210","feedback":"p-hat is the number of successes divided by the sample size: 210/1000."}],"multi":false,"hint":"210 out of 1,000.","explanation":""},"key":"xetPIylsOn"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"Which sample results would count as evidence in favor of the alternative hypothesis?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Sample proportions much smaller than 0.23","feedback":"Correct! Since the alternative is one-sided (p < 0.23), only sample proportions well below 0.23 count as evidence for $H_a$."},{"correct":false,"text":"Sample proportions much larger than 0.23","feedback":"Sample proportions above 0.23 would, if anything, point away from this alternative."},{"correct":false,"text":"Sample proportions far from 0.23 in either direction","feedback":"With a one-sided alternative, only departures in the specified direction count."}],"multi":false,"hint":"$H_a$ says $p < 0.23$.","explanation":""},"key":"pE9eD9DKkQ"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Hypotheses and Sample Proportions","position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"key":"KvqgqkyU4U"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-hypotheses-and-sample-proportions","label":"Check Your Understanding: Hypotheses and Sample Proportions","html_id":"check-your-understanding-hypotheses-and-sample-proportions","implicit":true,"key":"HG7hTT0dUl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":122,"column":1},"end":{"line":122,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using data from 2008, the American Association of Community Colleges (AACC) reports that community college students constitute 46% of all U.S. undergraduates. Given the downturn in the U.S. economy, the AACC anticipates an increase in this percentage for 2010. A poll of 500 randomly chosen undergraduates taken in 2010 indicates that 52% are attending a community college.","position":{"start":{"line":122,"column":1},"end":{"line":122,"column":1}},"key":"Dv3yuxbjGn"}],"key":"rRx6KLNa7G"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the appropriate hypotheses for this study?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.46$; $H_a$: $p > 0.46$","feedback":"Correct! $H_0$: $p = 0.46$; $H_a$: $p > 0.46$, since an increase is anticipated."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.46$; $H_a$: $p \\neq 0.46$","feedback":"The AACC anticipates an increase, so the alternative should be one-sided (greater than)."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: $p = 0.52$; $H_a$: $p > 0.52$","feedback":"The null value should be the 2008 baseline 0.46, not the 2010 sample result 0.52."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The null value is the 2008 rate; the AACC anticipates an INCREASE.","explanation":""},"key":"o3xRDlU6VL"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-eb3d2b66f188eb0f5e15a57b7fcae6f3.mjs","model":{"question":"In this study, which value is the sample proportion and which is the null value?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Null value $p_0 = 0.46$; sample proportion p-hat = 0.52","feedback":"Correct! $p_0 = 0.46$ is the hypothesized (2008) value; p-hat = 0.52 is what the 2010 sample showed."},{"correct":false,"text":"Null value $p_0 = 0.52$; sample proportion p-hat = 0.46","feedback":"It's the reverse—0.46 is the baseline being tested, and 0.52 came from the sample."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The null value comes from the prior claim (2008); the sample proportion comes from the 2010 poll.","explanation":""},"key":"L4042u6POE"}],"key":"f3urr6D3iX"}],"key":"a0aSpmLFz2"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Overview Wrap-Up","url":"/pages/m15-overview-wrap-up","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"Step 1: Stating the Hypotheses for a Proportion","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-proporti-1","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}